  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-cust-search",
      "text": "[](LICENSE)\n[](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness)\n[](https://nodejs.org/)\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\nCustom Web Search plugin for DeepSeek Harness (DSH) WebUI. Searches through configured engines in order, automatically trying the next engine if one fails or times out.\n\n---"
    },
    {
      "section": "Core Features",
      "text": "- **Sequential Search**: Searches engines one by one in your configured order. If an engine encounters a network error, invalid key, or timeout, it automatically moves to the next enabled engine until it gets results.\n- **11 Search Engines Supported**: Built-in support for official DeepSeek bridge, free search engines (Bing, DuckDuckGo, SearXNG), and AI search engines (Tavily, Exa, Firecrawl, Parallel, Perplexity, etc.).\n- **Model-facing excerpts preserved**: DSH 0.2.2 split a source's text into a display `snippet` and a longer `summary` for the model. The plugin forwards both, so the official DeepSeek Search API's summaries reach `web_search` at their full length instead of being flattened to a 300-character snippet.\n- **Readiness-aware tool visibility**: the provider implements the optional `ready()` hook, so a chain whose every enabled engine is missing its credential hides `web_search` rather than failing after the model calls it."
    },
    {
      "section": "Core Features",
      "text": "---"
    },
    {
      "section": "Supported Search Engines",
      "text": "| Engine | ID | Type | Credential | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **DeepSeek Official** | `deepseek` | `Bridge` | Official plugin settings | Bridge to the official providers: `deepseek-search-api` (direct Search API) preferred, `deepseek-official` (Messages `web_search`) as fallback |\n| **Bing** | `bing` | `Free` | None | Region/locale-driven web search |\n| **DuckDuckGo** | `ddg` | `Free` | None | HTML primary channel with Lite fallback |\n| **SearXNG** | `searxng` | `Free` | None | Rotates public instances or custom endpoints |\n| **Tavily** | `tavily` | `Keyed` | `TAVILY_API_KEY` | High-quality API when keyed, shared channel when keyless |\n| **AnySearch** | `anysearch` | `Free` | None | Keyless structured AI search |\n| **Exa** | `exa` | `Keyed` | `EXA_API_KEY` | Neural search; REST when keyed, public MCP when keyless |\n| **Keenable** | `keenable` | `Keyed` | `KEENABLE_API_KEY` | Real-time search; REST when keyed, free MCP when keyless |\n| **Firecrawl** | `firecrawl` | `Keyed` | `FIRECRAWL_API_KEY` | Deep scraping; anonymous quota when keyless |\n| **Parallel** | `parallel` | `Keyed` | `PARALLEL_API_KEY` | AI search; anonymous Streamable HTTP MCP when keyless |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Supported Search Engines",
      "text": "> The `deepseek` bridge reuses the official DeepSeek providers. DSH 0.2.2-rc.1 ships two: `deepseek-search-api` (one direct Search API request, no model turn, takes a result count) and `deepseek-official` (a Messages turn with the native `web_search` server tool). The bridge prefers the direct API and falls back to Messages search, skipping a registered provider only while another one reports itself usable — with neither configured it still picks one, so the missing-key error names the key instead of claiming nothing is registered. `@deepseek-ai/dsh-web` exposes no public \"provider by id\" accessor, so the bridge reads the web runtime's provider registry directly (one adapter, `src/server/engines/deepseek.ts`) and degrades to \"engine unavailable\" if that internal shape changes."
    },
    {
      "section": "Supported Search Engines",
      "text": "---"
    },
    {
      "section": "DSH Version Compatibility",
